Tests.Prompt:
Analyze the following Dependency Injection (DI) configuration in ASP.NET Core:

public class OrderProcessingService {
    private readonly AppDbContext _dbContext;
    public OrderProcessingService(AppDbContext dbContext) => _dbContext = dbContext;
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain the danger of 'Captive Dependencies' when a Singleton service captures a Scoped service (such as DbContext), leading to Memory Leaks, non-thread-safe DbContext operations, and stale data.
2. Show both valid refactorings: changing 'OrderProcessingService' to Scoped or injecting 'IServiceScopeFactory' / 'IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>' to create temporary scopes.

An ASP.NET Core system uses two manual caching layers:

public async Task<ProductDto?> GetProductAsync(int id) {
    var key = $"product:{id}";
    if (_memCache.TryGetValue(key, out ProductDto? cached)) return cached;

    var bytes = await _distCache.GetAsync(key);
    if (bytes != null) {
        cached = Deserialize<ProductDto>(bytes);
        _memCache.Set(key, cached, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));
        return cached;
    }

    cached = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);
    await _distCache.SetAsync(key, Serialize(cached), new DistributedCacheEntryOptions { AbsoluteExpirationRelativeToNow = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(30) });
    _memCache.Set(key, cached, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));
    return cached;
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain the limitations of the manual pattern (cache stampede / thundering herd, manual serialization, double invalidation) that HybridCache resolves.
2. Refactor the method using the 'Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Hybrid.HybridCache' API (GetOrCreateAsync) with LocalExpiration and DistributedExpiration options.

